

-- Startup --
When the FOREGROUND flag is set to 0 or false, the server prints a few indicator messages to the terminal, then correctly detaches from the terminal, 
closes all file descriptors. Messages are sent to a file called bbserv.log, which resides in the run/ subdirectory. The tests below were performed with 
PDEBUG enabled.

Testing WRITE command using <WRITE Hello, World>:
- Client receives "3.0 WROTE 1",
- bbserv.log contains message "master-client: 3.0 WROTE 1",
- bbfile contains message "1/Dennis/Hello, World"

Testing REPLACE command using <REPLACE 1 Replace, World>:
- Client receives "4.0 REPLACED 1",
- bbserv.log contains "master-client: 4.0 REPLACED 1",
- bbfile contains "1/Dennis/Replace, World".

Testing <QUIT>:
- Client receives "9.0 BYE Goodbye".

Testing PID locking:
- One instance of the server is launched from a terminal window and its process id was recorded as 9465. Three other instance of the server were then launched but 
  only process id 9465 was shown. bbserv.log contained the following messages from the test:
  - daemonize: cannot lock pid file: Resource temporarily unavailable
  - daemonize: cannot lock pid file: Resource temporarily unavailable
  - daemonize: cannot lock pid file: Resource temporarily unavailable
- bbserv.pid_file, the file responsible for storing the PID of the currently running server contained the process id 9465.


-- Dynamic Reconfiguration --
Dynamic Reconfiguration was tested with the FOREGROUND flag set to true.

Testing SIGHUP Reconfiguration:
- Before Reconfiguration:
  - Configuration is printed "thmax: 5, thincr: 3, bbport: 8000, fdebug: 0, bbfile: bbfile forground: 1, pdebug: 1, rport: 9000".
  - A total of 5 threads are counted. This is correct as the server spawns thincr threads alongside the main process thread and the replication listener thread.

- Reconfiguration:
  - THMAX, THINCR, BBPORT, and RPORT are then set to 10, 6, 9000, 9050, respectively. The server is then sent a SIGHUP signal using <kill -HUP pid>.
  - The server prints out "SIGHUP signal handled. reconfiguring server".
  - The server prints out "thmax: 10, thincr: 6, bbport: 9000, fdebug: 0, bbfile: bbfile forground: 1, pdebug: 1, rport: 9050".
  - A total of 8 threads are counted. This is correct (thincr + main + replication listener).

Client Functionality:
- <telnet localhost 8000> fails to connect to the server.
- <telnet localhost 9000> successfully connects to the server and can perform the same READ, WRITE, REPLACE, and QUIT operations.

Testing SIGINT:
- Entering CTRL+C in the terminal causes the server terminate after a two second wait for all clients to timeout.

-- Replication Algorithm --
For the tests below, the master (serverA) was assigned BBPORT 8000, RPORT 9000, PEER localhost:9001, BBFILE bbfileA. The slave (serverB) 
was given BBPORT 8001, RPORT 9001, PEER localhost 9000, BBFILE bbfileB. The client connects to the master server using telnet localhost 8000.
PDEBUG was enabled for the tests to validate communication. The three server config files used for testing are provided in the root directory.

Testing WRITE command using WRITE Hello, Write:
- Messages Exchanged:
  - Master broadcasts "PRECOM SYN", 
  - Peer responds with "ACK", 
  - Master broadcasts "COM WRITE|1|Alice|-1|Hello, Write".
  - Peer responds with "ACK",
  - Master broadcasts "OK".
  - Master sends "3.0 WROTE 1" to client.

- BBFILES:
  - bbfileA is appended with the string provided by the client.
  - bbfileB is appended with the string provided by the client.
  - bbfileA and bbfileB contain the same contents.


Testing REPLACE command using REPLACE 1 Hello, Replace:
- Messages Exchanged:
  - Master broadcasts "PRECOM SYN",
  - Peer responds with "ACK",
  - Master broadcasts "COM REPLACE|2|Alice|1|Hello, Replace",
  - Peer responds with "ACK",
  - Master broadcasts "OK".
  - Master sends client "4.0 REPLACED 1".

- BBFILES:
  - bbfileA message 1 "Hello, Write" is replaced with "Hello, Replace".
  - bbfileB message 1 "Hello, Write" is replaced with "Hello, Replace".


Testing invalid REPLACE command using REPLACE 100 Hello, Fail:
- Messages Exchanged:
  - Master broadcasts "PRECOM SYN",
  - Peer responds with "ACK",
  - Master broadcasts "COM REPLACE|2|Alice|100|Hello, Fail",
  - Peer responds with "NAK",
  - Master broadcasts "NOK REPLACE",
  - Master sends client "4.1 ERROR REPLACE COMMIT phase failed".
   
- BBFILES:
  - No modifications were made to the bbfiles.

Testing rollback functionality:
To test rollback functionality, I added a new serverC.conf file with RPORT 9002, PEER localhost:9000 and added localhost:9002 to the master server PEER
list. serverC's bbfile was manually modified so that a REPLACE operation from the client would fail on its side but succeed on the other slaves side. 
The replace command used in this test case was REPLACE 2 Hello, Rollback.

- Messages Exchanged:
  - Master broadcasts "PRECOM SYN",
  - Peer 0 responds with "ACK",
  - Peer 1 responds with "ACK",
  - Master broadcasts "COM REPLACE|3|anonymous coward|2|Hello, Rollback",
  - Peer 0 responds with "ACK",
  - Peer 1 responds with "NAK",
  - Master broadcasts "NOK REPLACE",
  - Master sends client "4.1 ERROR REPLACE COMMIT phase failed".

- BBFILES:
  - ServerB, the peer that successfully replaced its "Hello, Rollback" message to the previous message.

Testing unready peer:
- To ensure that the precommit phase fails if a peer is unavailable, the server was launched with two peers and one was terminated. Upon sending a WRITE operation 
from the client, the message "master broadcasting: ABRT" was broadcast and the client received "3.3 ERROR WRITE failure during PRECOMMIT phase".






